/** @page i18ncasemapping Case Mapping and Capitalization

    @section i18ncasemappingintro Case Mapping

    Case mappings are locale-sensitive.  Qore's own \c upr() and \c lwr() apply the locale-independent
    Unicode mappings, which are right nearly everywhere and wrong in the places that matter most to the
    people who live there: Turkish and Azeri case the dotted and dotless I as distinct letters, and Dutch
    capitalizes the \c ij digraph as a unit.

    @code{.py}
%requires i18n

printf("%s\n", I18n::to_upper("isim", "tr"));      # İSİM
printf("%s\n", I18n::to_upper("isim", "en"));      # ISIM
printf("%s\n", I18n::to_lower("ILETISIM", "tr"));  # ıletısım
printf("%s\n", I18n::capitalize("ijsselmeer", "nl")); # IJsselmeer
printf("%s\n", I18n::capitalize("ijsselmeer", "en")); # Ijsselmeer
    @endcode

    The Greek final sigma is positional rather than a separate case mapping, and it is produced where it
    belongs without any manual substitution:

    @code{.py}
printf("%s\n", I18n::to_lower("ΟΔΟΣ", "el")); # οδος, ending in U+03C2
    @endcode

    @section i18ncasemappingfull Full and Simple Mappings

    Full case mappings can change the length of a string: upper-casing the German sharp s yields two
    letters, because it has no single-character upper-case form.  Pass \c simple for one-to-one mappings,
    which return such a letter unchanged.

    @code{.py}
printf("%s\n", I18n::to_upper("ß", "de"));                     # SS
printf("%s\n", I18n::to_upper("ß", "de", {"simple": True}));   # ß
    @endcode

    The distinction is the reliable way to ask whether a letter is cased at all: a lower-case letter has a
    single-character upper-case form only if it genuinely carries case, so the sharp s and the typographic
    ligatures answer no.

    @section i18ncasemappingtitle Titlecasing Is Not Title Case

    @ref Qore::I18n::to_title() "to_title()" applies the Unicode titlecase operation, capitalizing the first
    letter of every word.  That is a character operation, <b>not</b> the typographic title case of any
    language, and the names collide unhelpfully.  Use it to normalize a name or a shouted string:

    @code{.py}
printf("%s\n", I18n::to_title("JOHN SMITH", "en"));             # John Smith
printf("%s\n", I18n::to_title("wait for active shards", "en")); # Wait For Active Shards -- every word
    @endcode

    To capitalize a label or a heading the way a locale expects, use
    @ref Qore::I18n::to_display_case() "to_display_case()" instead.

    @section i18ncasemappingfold Case Folding

    Case folding produces a form for caseless matching.  It is locale-independent by definition and
    therefore takes no locale; the result is a comparison key, not display text.

    @code{.py}
printf("%y\n", I18n::fold_case("straße") == I18n::fold_case("STRASSE")); # True
    @endcode

    @section i18ncasemappingdisplay Capitalization Convention

    Languages disagree about how a short label or heading is capitalized.  English uses title case and
    leaves a function word in lower case unless it begins or ends the phrase; most other languages
    capitalize only the first word; scripts without case have no such convention at all.

    @code{.py}
printf("%s\n", I18n::to_display_case("wait for active shards", "en")); # Wait for Active Shards
printf("%s\n", I18n::to_display_case("created at", "en"));             # Created At -- "at" ends it
printf("%s\n", I18n::to_display_case("numero de commande", "fr"));     # Numero de commande
printf("%s\n", I18n::to_display_case("支払い番号", "ja"));               # unchanged
    @endcode

    Initial letters are capitalized and the remainder of each word is left as written, so acronyms already
    present in the phrase survive.  The one exception is an English function word between the first and last
    words, which is lower-cased because the convention requires it.

    The same data is available directly, for a caller that renders each word itself:

    @code{.py}
printf("%s\n", I18n::get_capitalization_style("en")); # title
printf("%s\n", I18n::get_capitalization_style("fr")); # sentence
printf("%s\n", I18n::get_capitalization_style("ja")); # preserve
printf("%y\n", I18n::get_function_words("en"));       # ("a", "an", "and", ...)
printf("%y\n", I18n::get_function_words("fr"));       # () -- not a title-style locale
    @endcode

    @note These capitalization styles are Qore data rather than CLDR data.  CLDR carries capitalization
    context only for its own display names, through \c contextTransforms and the display-name and formatter
    APIs, and that data describes how a locale capitalizes nouns in those names rather than how it
    capitalizes a heading; read as a heading-style signal it inverts the answer for English, whose language
    names are always capitalized.

    @note This layer applies a language convention and knows nothing of technical or brand vocabulary; it
    renders \c "api key" as \c "Api key".  Vocabulary of that kind is domain data rather than language data,
    and belongs to the caller: use @ref Qore::I18n::get_capitalization_style() "get_capitalization_style()"
    and @ref Qore::I18n::get_function_words() "get_function_words()" to drive a per-word rendering that
    applies both.

    @note A locale with no entry uses sentence case, the majority convention.  The root locale uses title
    case so that a caller passing @ref Qore::I18n::get_thread_locale() "get_thread_locale()" through with no
    locale set does not silently move existing English labels to sentence case.
*/
